<problem>
<!-- <title>Definite integral &#x2D; exponential</title> -->

<setup>

<selectFromSequence name="a" from="-10" to="10" exclude="0" numToSelect="2" withReplacement="true" />

<function name="f" variables="t" simplify="true" symbolic="true"><math simplify="numbers">$a[1] *e^($a[2] *t)</math></function>
<selectFromSequence name="lb" from="-2" to="1" />
<selectFromSequence name="ub" from="$lb+1" to="2" />

<function name="antideriv_f" variables="t" simplify="numbers" symbolic="true">$a[1] e^($a[2]*t)/$a[2]</function>

<math name="intfeval" simplify="numbers">$$antideriv_f($ub)-$$antideriv_f($lb)</math>

</setup>

<p>
Evaluate the following integral:</p>
  
<p><m>\displaystyle \int_{$lb}^{$ub} $f\, dt  =</m> 
<answer forceFullCheckworkButton="true">
<award symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="true">$intfeval </award>
<award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.0001" credit="0.8" name="award1">$intfeval </award>
<award credit="0.64" allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001" feedbackCodes="numericalerror" name="award2">$intfeval </award>
</answer>
</p>
<feedback condition="$award1 or $award2">For full credit, don't round.  Use e^() or exp() and leave fractions as fractions.</feedback>

<copy prop="feedbacks" source="answer1" />

<p>

For full credit, do not round your answer to a decimal.  (Maximum of 80% credit will be awarded for rounded answers that include at least 5 correct significant digits.)
</p>


<solution>
<setup>
  <function name="antideriv_f_part" variables="t" simplify="numbers" symbolic="true">e^($a[2]*t)/$a[2]</function>
  
</setup>
  
<p>We first use the constant product rule for integration: 
</p>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle  \int_{$lb}^{$ub} $f\, dt  = $a[1]   \int_{$lb}^{$ub}<math simplify="numbers">e^($a[2] *t)</math> dt</m>
</p>
<p>
We then find the antiderivative:
</p>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle $a[1] \left( $antideriv_f_part \right) </m>
</p>
<p>
Evaluating at the bounds gives us:
</p>
<p>
<m>\displaystyle $$antideriv_f($ub) - $$antideriv_f($lb)  = $intfeval </m>
</p>


</solution>


</problem>